AI Governance Overlay for CGRC Risk Management
Objectives
By completing this lab, you will:

Configure a Python-based AI risk register on a Linux system
Map an AI use case to NIST AI RMF profiles (Govern, Map, Measure, Manage)
Identify algorithmic harms, bias vectors, and data lineage gaps for a black-box ML system
Cross-map NIST AI 600-1 controls to SP 800-53 control families within a CGRC RMF package
Produce an AI risk treatment plan and AO briefing artifact
Prerequisites
Working knowledge of NIST RMF (Categorize, Select, Implement, Assess, Authorize, Monitor)
Familiarity with SP 800-53 control families and CGRC authorization packages
Python 3.10+ scripting proficiency (JSON/YAML handling, CLI tools)
Conceptual understanding of ML model risk (bias, drift, explainability)
Basic Git and Linux CLI competency
Environment Setup
Single Linux machine (provided via Start Lab). No cloud dependencies required.

sudo apt update && sudo apt install -y python3-pip python3-venv git jq
mkdir -p ~/ai-governance-lab/{register,profiles,evidence,artifacts}
cd ~/ai-governance-lab
python3 -m venv venv && source venv/bin/activate
pip install pandas pyyaml jsonschema tabulate
Clone reference control catalogs (mirrors are acceptable if NIST source is unreachable):

# SP 800-53 Rev 5 OSCAL catalog (JSON)
git clone --depth 1 https://github.com/usnistgov/oscal-content.git
ls oscal-content/nist.gov/SP800-53/rev5/json/
Retrieve or manually transcribe the NIST AI RMF Playbook and AI 600-1 (Generative AI Profile) control text into profiles/ai_600-1_controls.yaml — no official machine-readable OSCAL exists yet, so you must structure this yourself.

Scenario
Your organization is deploying an AI-enabled clinical decision-support tool derived from a BCI signal-classification model (black-box neural network) that flags anomalous neural activity for physician review. You are the AI Governance Lead building the CGRC authorization package overlay.

